  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 110 PM EDT Wed Sep 2 2026\n\nHeat and humidity persist today with high temperatures expected to \nbe in the upper 80s to low 90s. The upper level ridge is directly \nover the Great Lakes with a large area of surface high pressure \nanchored over the southeastern CONUS. Ample WAA will continue to \nboost temperatures well above average for early September. Increased \nmoisture return stemming from the Gulf will also allow for dewpoints \nin the mid 70s to continue. The Heat Advisory continues for the \nentire forecast area until 8 PM EDT; it has been extended to last \nuntil 8 PM EDT Friday for counties along and south of US 30 in \nIndiana and Ohio. Confidence is growing that heat indices will \nbe at or slightly above 100 degrees tomorrow in the vicinity of \nthe US 30 corridor, where there are lower chances for convection\nand/or clouds. We should stay mainly dry today; originally \nthere was a low chance for a stray storm but given that an \noutflow morning from remnant morning upstream convection is \nalready coming through and there is a downward trend on visible \nsatellite imagery for cloud cover, have opted to keep the area \ndry today and tonight. \n\nAs the upper level ridge begins to flatten, our Lower Michigan \ncounties may be on the periphery. Have maintained 15-20% chances for \nisolated convection between 06-18Z mainly north of I-80/I-90, with \nbroader 15-20% expanding into the entire area after 18Z Thursday\nin line with the 12Z suite of CAMs. Thursday's PoPs are highly \nconditional depending on where/if any outflows or shortwaves \nsetup. Depending on how much the ridge flattens and where the \nbest convective environment sets up, our area may see an active \npattern to end the week into the weekend ahead. Hi- res and \nmedium range guidance suggests one or multiple MCSs could move \nthrough the area late Thursday night through Saturday. Model \nguidance continues to be all over the place in regards to timing\nand exact location of storms Thursday through the weekend, \nhowever, in general, our hot and humid environment overhead will\nbe supportive for convection. We should have ample instability \nand moisture to work with but other elements such as shear \nremain in question due to considerable uncertainty on how the \nlarger synoptic evolution.\n\nAs mentioned in the previous discussion, there is a large spread on \nthe long range temperatures and evolution of the upper level ridge. \nWhile the NBM has highs near 80 by late in the weekend into early \nnext week, there is still about a 10 degree spread in guidance \nranging from 75-85 degrees for highs. With troughing expected to \nsetup over the western CONUS, the upper level ridge may briefly \ndiminish over the weekend and then build back into the area early \ninto the middle of next week.",
